AUDIO: 'simple human experience. Let\'s chat about the experience of using Astra. First up, I used Astra inside of Codex, and I recommend you do the same. Codex is honestly amazing. I prefer it over'

[18:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Claude code. I pretty much prefer it over any other agentic platform that can actually build and do things on your Mac or PC. It really brings the best out of this model and really any other OpenAI model. Honestly, when comparing GPT-6'

[18:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Astra to 5.6 Soul, the immediate differences don\'t jump out to you at first. Soul was extremely capable, and given its lower price point for folks on the plus plan, in a lot of tasks, it'

[18:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'might actually be the preferred option. Not just Soul, but even GPT-5.6 Luna, surprisingly agentically capable. If you start to use GPT-6 Astra and don\'t notice a huge jump in capability up'

[19:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'front, your task might actually just not be difficult enough to really bring out the breadth of the model compared to the previous 5.6 tier. Take for example, a simple task of installing some video'

[19:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'game mods. This is something I\'ve done with Luna, Soul, and Astra. It gets the job done in this case pretty quick, but Luna probably could have done the same thing pretty much just as fast. Astra is, yes, far more capable, but you'

[19:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really have to push it to start seeing the big differences. And in my experience, one of those big differences is spinning up more sub-agents and using them more efficiently. You could see this particular game dev task used a'

[19:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'total of 16 different agents, everything from review to building terrain. Codex\'s harness for maintaining long-term context was already really good. I honestly didn\'t notice a significant'

[20:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bump or difference inside of Codex. Also, similar to Soul, I noticed that when giving it a prompt with a massively wide scope, it still seems to fixate on certain areas and ignore other parts of the prompt. It\'s not a case of'

[20:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forgetting. It\'s more of a case of fixation on certain parts of the prompt that it has naturally deemed the most important up front. It\'s not necessarily the most amazing, similar to Soul and other language models, at orchestrating'

[20:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'itself out of the box. This especially rings true when working on a massive project. Explicitly explaining what you want for each pass and seeding it with ideas to increase its efficiency. For example, giving each sub-agent more'

[20:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work, asking it to track time spent in certain areas of the project, and giving it the freedom to drop a certain portion in favor of another more important one. The model to me doesn\'t feel vastly more token hungry than GPT 5.6 Soul. In fact,'

[20:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the light and medium thinking modes, it\'s very efficient and can be faster than Soul. Massive coding projects seem to use similar token amounts. I absolutely noticed a bump in usage cost.'

[21:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It drains your usage faster in Codex. The model is simply more expensive. Codex is super efficient with its tokens, caching a vast amount of them. Still though, I burned through those'

[21:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resets like you wouldn\'t believe, hammering the model almost around the clock. Let\'s talk about computer use, too, because this is one of the main things they advertised. It\'s definitely a little bit better. Still honestly'

[21:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pretty slow. But yes, better than Soul, and it\'s better at problem solving when it clicks on something and what it expected to happen doesn\'t. Computer use is great, and it can get a lot done, but I don\'t understand why OpenAI won\'t'

[21:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'allow it to initiate several actions in computer use at once. I would love to see the day where it can initiate strings of actions at once that happen quickly. Oh, and without completely relinquishing me of control over the'

